EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     EExxaammppllee 11: Simple conversion of units

       This example shows how to do simple conversions, for example from giga‐
       bytes to bytes:

         $ uunniittss --oo %%00..ff --tt ''44 ggiiggaabbyytteess'' bbyytteess
         4294967296

       The --oo %%00..ff part of the command is required to print the result in a
       non-scientific notation (e.g, 4294967296 instead of 4.29497e+09).

     EExxaammppllee 22: Interactive usage

       Here is an example of an interactive session where the user is prompted
       for units:

         You have: mmeetteerrss
         You want: ffeeeett
                 * 3.2808399
                 / 0.3048

         You have: ccmm^^33
         You want: ggaalllloonnss
                 * 0.00026417205
                 / 3785.4118

         You have: mmeetteerrss//ss
         You want: ffuurrlloonnggss//ffoorrttnniigghhtt
                 * 6012.8848
                 / 0.00016630952

         You have: 11||22 iinncchh
         You want: ccmm
                 * 1.27
                 / 0.78740157

         You have: 8855 ddeeggFF
         You want: ddeeggCC
                 29.444444

     EExxaammppllee 33: Difference between “||” and “//” division

       The following command shows how to convert half a meter to centimeters.

         $ uunniittss ''11||22 mmeetteerr'' ccmm
                 * 50
                 / 0.02

       uunniittss prints the expected result because the division operator for num‐
       bers (“|”) was used.

       Using the division operator for units (“/”) would result in an error:

         $ uunniittss ''11//22 mmeetteerr'' ccmm
         conformability error
                 0.5 / m
                 0.01 m

       It is because uunniittss interprets “11//22 mmeetteerr” as “00..55//mmeetteerr”, which is not
       conformable to “ccmm”.

     EExxaammppllee 44: Simple units file
       Here is an example of a short units file that defines some basic units:

         m         !a!
         sec       !b!
         micro-    1e-6
         minute    60 sec
         hour      60 min
         inch      0.0254 m
         ft        12 inches
         mile      5280 ft

     EExxaammppllee 55: Viewing units and conversions of the default units file
       The following shell one-liner allows the user to view the contents of
       the default units file:

         $ lleessss ""$$((uunniittss --UU))""

